\title{Flow Value Lemma}
\date{2026-11-16T18:50:01Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}
\import{flow-macros}

\taxon{Lemma}
\p{Let #{f} be any [flow](00RZ) and let #{(A,B)} be any [#{s}-#{t} cut](00SO) in a [flow network](00RX) #{G}. Then the [net flow](00SR) of #{(A,B)} with respect to #{f} equals the [value](00S0) of #{f}. That is, ##{v(A,B) = v(f).}}

\proof{
\stmt{#{v(f)}}
\reason{=}{[Definition of flow value](00S0)}
\stmt{#{\fout(s)}}
\reason{=}{#{\fin(s) = 0} since #{s} must have indegree #{0}}
\stmt{#{\fout(s) - \fin(s)}}
\reason{=}{By definition of a [flow](00RZ), #{\fout(v) - \fin(v) = 0} for every #{v \in A} except for #{s}}
\stmt{#{\sum_{v \in A} (\fout(v) - \fin(v))}}
\reason{=}{See explanation below}
\stmt{#{\sum_{e : A \to B} f(e)} - \sum_{e : B \to A} f(e)}
\reason{=}{definition of [net flow](00SR)}
\stmt{#{v(A,B)}}

\p{The central step involves rearranging the sum by edge instead of by vertex.  If an edge lies completely within #{A}, then its flow will get added once (for its source vertex) and subtracted once (for its target vertex), and hence the flow will cancel out.  If an edge lies completely within #{B}, its flow will not contribute to the sum at all.  On the other hand, if an edge goes from #{A} to #{B}, then its flow is added once; if from #{B} to #{A}, then its flow is only subtracted once.  This results in the expression on the following line.}
}
